HISTORY OF LINUX

🟩 1. Before Linux — Unix (1969–1991)

In 1969, Ken Thompson, Dennis Ritchie, and others at AT&T Bell Labs created UNIX.

UNIX introduced:

Multi-user support

File permissions

Shell commands

Portable C language (also from Dennis Ritchie)



📌 UNIX became the father of almost all modern OS systems (Linux, MacOS, Windows etc.).


---

🟩 2. GNU Project (1983)

Richard Stallman started the GNU Project aiming to build a free (open-source) UNIX-like OS.

GNU created:

Bash shell

GCC compiler

Libraries

Core utilities



❗But GNU did not have a working kernel.
The kernel project, Hurd, was slow.


---

🟩 3. Birth of Linux Kernel (1991)

Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old student from Finland, started writing a small kernel “just for fun”.

On August 25, 1991, he posted on the internet:


> “I am doing a free operating system… just a hobby, won’t be big.”



But it became huge.

He released Linux Kernel 0.01 in September 1991.

It was open-source and anyone could modify it.



---

🟩 4. Linux + GNU = A Complete OS

GNU had:

Shell

Tools

Compilers


Linux provided:

Kernel (the heart of OS)


Together they became:

👉 GNU/Linux Operating System


