Linux services are background programs that start automatically and keep running to perform important tasks on a Linux system.

Think of services as helpers that work silently in the background to keep your system or server running smoothly.


---

✅ Simple Definition

A Linux service is a program that runs in the background (daemon) to perform continuous tasks like networking, logging, security, web hosting, etc.

These services are usually managed using:

systemctl (modern Linux)

service (older Linux)



---

🔹 Examples of Linux Services

Service Name	What It Does

sshd	Allows SSH remote login
httpd / nginx	Runs web server
mysqld	MySQL database server
docker	Runs Docker containers
crond	Executes scheduled cron jobs
firewalld	Manages firewall rules
network / NetworkManager	Manages network connections
rsyslog	Handles system logging



---

🔹 Checking a service status

systemctl status sshd


---

🔹 Start a service

sudo systemctl start nginx


---

🔹 Enable a service (start automatically on boot)

sudo systemctl enable docker


---

🔹 List all services

systemctl list-units --type=service


---

🧠 Why are Linux services important?

Because on servers, everything runs as a service, like:

Web servers

Databases

Firewalls

Monitoring agents

Container engines

CI/CD agents


Without services, your server cannot function properly.
