A library in computing is a collection of pre-written code that you can reuse in your programs so you don’t have to write everything from scratch.

Think of it like a toolbox containing ready-made tools.


---

🧠 Simple Definition

A library = pre-written functions + code + modules that developers use to make work easier and faster.


---

🔹 Why libraries are used

Save time

Avoid writing repeated code

Use tested and reliable functions

Make programs smaller and cleaner



---

🔹 Real Examples

1. In Python

import math

Here math is a library that gives functions like sin(), cos(), sqrt(), etc.


---

2. In Linux

Linux uses two types of libraries:

✔️ Static Libraries (.a files)

Linked directly into the program.

✔️ Shared Libraries (.so files)

Loaded at runtime.
Example:
libc.so → standard C library used by almost every program in Linux.


---

3. In Windows

Libraries end with .dll
Example:
kernel32.dll → system functions
user32.dll → GUI functions


---

🔹 Types of Libraries

1. System Libraries

Used by OS (Linux/Windows).

2. Programming Libraries

Used in code (Python, Java, C, C++).

3. Software Libraries

Used by applications (graphics, databases, etc.)
