A dependency is anything that your software or system needs in order to work.

Think of it like a supporting part.
If the dependency is missing, the main program won’t run properly.


---

🧠 Simple Definition

A dependency is a library, package, service, or component that another software depends on.


---

🔹 Easy Real-Life Example

If you want to drive a car:

Fuel is a dependency

Tyres are a dependency

Engine oil is a dependency


Without them, the car won’t run.


---

🔹 Technical Example (Software)

If you install a Python program that uses the requests library:

requests

is a dependency
The program needs it to send HTTP requests.


---

🔹 In Linux

If you install nginx, it may require:

openssl

pcre

zlib


These are dependencies.
Linux automatically installs them.


---

🔹 In DevOps

Tools depend on each other:

Jenkins depends on Java

Docker depends on containerd

Kubernetes depends on etcd, kubelet, container runtime


If dependencies are missing → errors happen.


---

🔹 Types of Dependencies

Type	Meaning

Library dependency	Needs a .so, .dll, or library
Package dependency	Needs another package to be installed
Service dependency	Needs a service to run first (example: MySQL depends on systemd-networkd)
Version dependency	Needs a specific version (Python 3.10, Java 17, etc.)




