🗂️ LINUX FILESYSTEM HIERARCHY – DETAILED + ONE-LINE MEMORY SHORTCUTS


---

🔵 1. / — Root Directory

Detailed:
The top-level directory of the entire Linux filesystem. All files and folders exist under /, even other mounted disks.
Shortcut:
✔ / = "Starting point of the entire Linux world."


---

🔵 2. /bin — Essential User Commands

Detailed:
Contains basic user-level programs that are needed for the system to run even in single-user mode.
Examples: ls, cat, mkdir, cp.
Shortcut:
✔ /bin = "Basic commands every user needs."


---

🔵 3. /sbin — System Admin Commands

Detailed:
Contains administrative commands used for system maintenance, mainly for the root user.
Examples: reboot, fdisk, ifconfig, ip.
Shortcut:
✔ /sbin = "System binaries for admins."


---

🔵 4. /usr — User Applications + Libraries

Detailed:
Second most important directory. Contains installed software, libraries, manuals, shared files.
Sub-directories:

/usr/bin → Most commands

/usr/sbin → Admin commands

/usr/lib → Libraries

/usr/share → Common data
Shortcut:
✔ /usr = "Installed software lives here."



---

🔵 5. /var — Variable Data

Detailed:
Contains files that grow or change frequently.
Examples:

/var/log → Logs

/var/www → Web content

/var/lib → DB files

/var/spool → Email queue
Shortcut:
✔ /var = “Varies — logs and data that keep growing.”



---

🔵 6. /etc — Configuration Files

Detailed:
Contains all system configuration files.
No binaries here. Only configs.
Examples:
/etc/passwd, /etc/ssh/sshd_config, /etc/fstab.
Shortcut:
✔ /etc = "Everything To Configure."


---

🔵 7. /home — User Home Directories

Detailed:
Each user gets a folder to store personal files, settings, downloads, etc.
Example: /home/mahendra.
Shortcut:
✔ /home = "Users’ personal homes."


---

🔵 8. /root — Root User's Home

Detailed:
Separate home directory for the root (superuser).
It is NOT under /home for security reasons.
Shortcut:
✔ /root = "Home of the superuser."


---

🔵 9. /boot — Bootloader Files

Detailed:
Contains the Linux kernel, initrd, and GRUB configuration — everything required to start the OS.
Examples: vmlinuz, initrd.img, /boot/grub/.
Shortcut:
✔ /boot = “Files needed to boot Linux.”


---

🔵 10. /lib, /lib64 — System Libraries

Detailed:
Contains shared libraries required by /bin and /sbin (similar to DLL files on Windows).
Shortcut:
✔ /lib = "System library files."


---

🔵 11. /dev — Device Files

Detailed:
Linux treats hardware as files. /dev contains special files for devices:
Examples:

/dev/sda → Hard disk

/dev/tty → Terminals

/dev/null → Black hole
Shortcut:
✔ /dev = "Devices represented as files."



---

🔵 12. /proc — Virtual Kernel + Process Info

Detailed:
Not real files. Kernel generates them in memory. Provides process and hardware info.
Examples:

/proc/cpuinfo

/proc/meminfo

/proc/1234 → running process
Shortcut:
✔ /proc = “Processes and kernel info in realtime.”



---

🔵 13. /sys — Modern Kernel/Hardware Interface

Detailed:
Similar to /proc but more structured. Used by system tools to interact with the kernel.
Shortcut:
✔ /sys = “System device info (modern proc).”


---

🔵 14. /tmp — Temporary Files

Detailed:
Used to store temporary files by apps and users.
Files here may be deleted on reboot.
Shortcut:
✔ /tmp = "Temporary stuff. Not permanent."


---

🔵 15. /opt — Optional Third-Party Software

Detailed:
Commercial and third-party software installed here.
Example:
/opt/google/, /opt/vscode.
Shortcut:
✔ /opt = “Optional apps installed manually.”


---

🔵 16. /media — Removable Media Mount Points

Detailed:
Used to automatically mount USB drives, CD/DVDs.
Examples:
/media/usb, /media/cdrom.
Shortcut:
✔ /media = “USB or CD mounts automatically.”


---

🔵 17. /mnt — Temporary Mount Point

Detailed:
Used by admins to manually mount partitions or storage.
Example:

mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt

Shortcut:
✔ /mnt = "Manual mount here."


---

🔵 18. /run — Runtime Data

Detailed:
Contains system runtime data since the last boot.
Example: pid files, sockets, service status.
Shortcut:
✔ /run = “Data created after boot.”


