---

## 4️⃣ CIDR Notation (/24, /26 etc.)

| CIDR | Subnet Mask   |
| ---- | ------------- |
| /8   | 255.0.0.0     |
| /16  | 255.255.0.0   |
| /24  | 255.255.255.0 |

**/24 means first 24 bits are network bits**

---

## 5️⃣ IP Calculation Example (/24)

### Given:

```
IP: 192.168.1.10/24
```

### Step-1: Subnet mask

```
/24 → 255.255.255.0
```

### Step-2: Network Address

```
192.168.1.0
```

### Step-3: Broadcast Address

```
192.168.1.255
```

### Step-4: Usable IPs

```
192.168.1.1  → 192.168.1.254
```

### Step-5: Total IPs

```
2^(32−24) = 256
Usable = 254
```

---

## 6️⃣ Subnetting (/25, /26, /27) – Easy Trick

### Formula

```
Number of IPs = 2^(32 − CIDR)
```

---

### Example 1️⃣: /26

```
32 − 26 = 6
2^6 = 64 IPs
```

Subnet mask:

```
255.255.255.192
```

---

### Subnet Range Example (/26)

Network: `192.168.1.0/26`

| Subnet | Range               |
| ------ | ------------------- |
| 1st    | 192.168.1.0 – 63    |
| 2nd    | 192.168.1.64 – 127  |
| 3rd    | 192.168.1.128 – 191 |
| 4th    | 192.168.1.192 – 255 |

---

## 7️⃣ Binary Method (Exam-Important)

### Subnet mask /26 in binary

```
11111111.11111111.11111111.11000000
```

Last octet:

```
11000000 = 192
```

---

## 8️⃣ Find Network Address (Binary AND)

### Example:

```
IP:   192.168.1.130
Mask: 255.255.255.192
```

Binary (last octet):

```
IP   = 10000010
MASK = 11000000
----------------
NET  = 10000000 = 128
```

### ✅ Network Address

```
192.168.1.128
```

---

## 9️⃣ Broadcast Address (Binary OR)

Host bits = all 1s

```
Network: 192.168.1.128
Broadcast: 192.168.1.191
```

