# 🌐 SSH vs Telnet – Complete Guide

---

## 1️⃣ Overview

| Feature        | SSH                          | Telnet                     |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Full Name      | Secure Shell                 | Telnet Protocol            |
| Port           | 22                           | 23                         |
| Security       | Encrypted (AES, RSA, etc.)   | Plain text (not secure)    |
| Authentication | Password / Key / Certificate | Password only              |
| Usage          | Remote management            | Remote management (legacy) |
| Preferred      | ✅                            | ❌ (deprecated)             |

---

## 2️⃣ Telnet – Basic

**Telnet** allows you to connect to a remote host **over TCP**, mainly for testing or legacy systems.

### 2.1 Install Telnet Client

```bash
# CentOS/RHEL
yum install telnet -y

# Ubuntu/Debian
apt install telnet -y
```

### 2.2 Basic Telnet Command

```bash
telnet <hostname or IP> <port>
```

Example:

```bash
telnet 192.168.1.10 23
```

### 2.3 Telnet Usage

* Check if a **port is open**:

```bash
telnet 192.168.1.10 80
```

* Connect to a **remote server** (login in plain text)

---

### ⚠️ Telnet Risks

* Sends username/password **in clear text**
* Vulnerable to sniffing and MITM attacks
* Mostly replaced by SSH

---

## 3️⃣ SSH – Basic

**SSH** is the **secure replacement for Telnet**.

### 3.1 Install SSH

```bash
# Server
yum install openssh-server -y
systemctl enable sshd
systemctl start sshd

# Client
yum install openssh-clients -y
```

### 3.2 Basic SSH Command

```bash
ssh username@hostname
```

Example:

```bash
ssh mahendra@192.168.1.10
```

---

### 3.3 Check SSH Status

```bash
systemctl status sshd
ss -tulpn | grep 22
```

---

## 4️⃣ SSH Authentication Methods

| Method      | Description                             |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Password    | Default login via password              |
| Key-based   | Public/Private key pair (more secure)   |
| Certificate | SSH certificates for large environments |

---

## 5️⃣ SSH Advanced – Key-Based Authentication

### 5.1 Generate Key Pair

```bash
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa
```

### 5.2 Copy Public Key to Server

```bash
ssh-copy-id user@server-ip
```

### 5.3 Connect Using Key

```bash
ssh user@server-ip
```

📌 No password prompt if key authentication works

---

## 6️⃣ SSH Advanced – Configuration

### 6.1 Config File (`/etc/ssh/sshd_config`)

Key parameters:

```text
Port 22             # Default port
PermitRootLogin no  # Disable root login
PasswordAuthentication yes/no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PermitEmptyPasswords no
AllowUsers mahendra admin
```

### 6.2 Restart SSH

```bash
systemctl restart sshd
```

---

### 6.3 Change Default Port (Security)

```bash
# Edit /etc/ssh/sshd_config
Port 2222
systemctl restart sshd
```

Connect using:

```bash
ssh -p 2222 user@host
```

---

## 7️⃣ SSH Advanced – Tunneling & Port Forwarding

| Type                               | Example                                   | Purpose                                      |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Local Port Forward                 | `ssh -L 8080:internalserver:80 user@jump` | Access internal webserver via localhost:8080 |
| Remote Port Forward                | `ssh -R 9090:localhost:22 user@server`    | Make local service accessible remotely       |
| Dynamic Port Forward (SOCKS proxy) | `ssh -D 1080 user@server`                 | Proxy all traffic via SSH                    |

---

## 8️⃣ SSH Advanced – Security Best Practices

1. **Disable root login**
2. **Use key-based auth only**
3. **Change default port**
4. **Enable firewall for SSH port**
5. **Use fail2ban** to block brute-force attacks
6. **Use ssh-agent** for key management

---

## 9️⃣ Telnet vs SSH Use Cases

| Use Case              | Telnet       | SSH           |
| --------------------- | ------------ | ------------- |
| Test network port     | ✅            | ✅             |
| Remote administration | ❌ (insecure) | ✅             |
| IoT / legacy devices  | ✅            | ❌             |
| Encrypted tunnel      | ❌            | ✅             |
| File transfer         | ❌            | ✅ (scp, sftp) |

---

## 🔟 SSH Related Tools

| Tool         | Purpose                     |
| ------------ | --------------------------- |
| `scp`        | Copy files securely via SSH |
| `sftp`       | Interactive file transfer   |
| `ssh-agent`  | Key management              |
| `ssh-keygen` | Generate SSH keys           |

---

## 11️⃣ Troubleshooting SSH

```bash
ssh -v user@host          # Verbose debug output
systemctl status sshd      # Check service
ss -tulpn | grep 22        # Check listening port
journalctl -u sshd         # Check logs
```

---

## 12️⃣ Summary

> **Telnet is a legacy, insecure protocol for remote access, now mostly replaced by SSH. SSH provides encrypted connections, key-based authentication, tunneling, and secure remote administration.**



